00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:32.700
Now, why do we do this? Why do we go
through this exercise?

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:44.700
Why is it actually important? Well,
cyclical commodities often move with the
business cycle, so with economic growth.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:51.700
The clue is in the name, cyclicality. They
move with the business cycle.

00:21:52.700 --> 00:22:05.700
So, if you think back to our other
business cycle indicators, so the ISM
manufacturing, ISM non-manufacturing, the
University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment
Index,

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:18.700
building permits, and the NFIB, and the
NFIB Small Business Optimism Index, if you
think of all of those business cycle
indicators that are leading indicators,

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:23.700
cyclical commodities essentially move with
them.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:33.700
They move and tend to lead as a gauge to
the business cycle.

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:50.700
The cyclical commodity prices themselves
are indicative of future CPI and PPI
levels, which are reported monthly, and
future economic growth, so real GDP
reported quarterly.

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:54.700
So, expansion and contraction, so
expansion and contraction in the economy.

00:22:54.700 --> 00:23:00.700
And of course, both real GDP and inflation
are reported after the fact.

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:09.700
Cyclical commodity prices are open quotes
leading close quotes because they are
trading and moving every day.

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:22.700
And then inflation is reported monthly,
and then economic growth, real GDP, is
reported quarterly, and they are both
reported after the fact.

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:23.700
So, let's think of an example here.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:41.700
So, a leading indicator like the ISM
manufacturing number may tell us at one
point in time, as traders, that there is a
turning point occurring in purchasing
manager expectations for the future.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:42.700
So, what would that look like?

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:43.700
So, what would that look like?

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:54.700
Well, they may be reporting new orders
growth after a contraction or growth is
accelerating, so there could be a turning
point or a trend.

00:23:54.700 --> 00:24:08.700
And this can be combined with, for
example, growth in inventory stocking and
increases, their reporting increases in
key input commodity prices to the
manufacturing process.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:09.700
So, what does that mean?

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:20.700
Well, it means we're getting higher
commodity prices, and we have to assume
here, as in the example, that we have a
constant supply of the commodity.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:22.700
So, supply is not moving.

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:25.700
So, we're concentrating on demand here.

00:24:25.700 --> 00:24:52.700
So, it means that there's higher commodity
prices given a constant supply in real
time, because businesses are buying more
input commodities, and this feeds into the
inflation statistics later, when they're
reported on a monthly basis, and prices,
by the time inflation is reported, have
already moved.

00:24:52.700 --> 00:25:07.700
So, what we need to understand with
cyclical commodities is what is a normal
and abnormal move, and use cyclical
commodity prices as a gauge to shifts in
inflation expectations.

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:14.700
Now, think in the bigger picture, what
we're trying to achieve here as traders
and PMs.

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:31.700
From a macro perspective, we are always
trying to gauge which way the wind is
blowing to enable a prediction of real GDP
growth and likely S&P 500 direction and
returns, and of course, potential Fed
policy.

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:32.700
Why do we do this?

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:32.700
Why do we do this?

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:33.700
Why do we do this?

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:36.700
Well, throughout the PTM video series,
it's been clear.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:41.700
We do this so we can position ourselves
with the right directional bias.

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:53.700
So, a portfolio of net long, net short, or
neutral positioning, and of course, with
the right longs and shorts in the
portfolio.

00:25:53.700 --> 00:26:10.700
So, this is how we use them as traders and
portfolio managers, how we use cyclical
commodities to make a more intelligent
decision around portfolio bias and
potentially what we want to be long and
short in a portfolio.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:21.700
So, we have to keep an eye on commodity
prices and inflation expectations and
understand them and interpret them and
what it all means at any one moment in
time.

00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:25.700
Now, we have to think about something else
as well.

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:37.700
So, for commodities themselves, there are
inventory dynamics, which has supply side
dynamics that we have to understand.

00:26:37.700 --> 00:26:43.700
So, there is a supply side caveat here
with cyclical commodities.

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:50.700
So, for commodities themselves, as
inventories are rising, this indicates a
rise in demand.

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:57.700
So, business or purchasing managers are
increasing demand and increasing their own
inventories.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:27:00.700
And we know that's a bullish indication.

00:27:00.700 --> 00:27:06.700
On the flip side, if inventories are
falling, this indicates a falling demand.

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:14.700
So, business and purchasing managers are
reducing their demand and reducing their
own inventories.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:18.700
And they're consciously doing this for
both cases.

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:24.700
So, rising inventories is indicating a
rise in demand from purchasing managers.

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:29.700
And that would be indicating bullish
conditions.

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:58.700
So, for these cyclical commodities in the
energy and industrial metals space, within
these companies, there are internally
traders of energy and industrial metals
commodities.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:07.700
And they are increasing or decreasing
their stocking levels in accordance with
actual and expected demand.

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:23.700
So, given a constant supply, prices that
are increasing and decreasing are a gauge
on business or purchasing managers' actual
and expectation of demand.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:27.700
But there is a caveat to all of this.

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:35.700
Prices are, of course, a function of both
demand and supply dynamics.

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:43.700
So, it's not an automatic function that
prices must follow expected demand.

00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:55.700
And, of course, there can also be cost
push or supply factors that we have to
consider as to why prices are where they
are at any one time.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:57.700
And we'll come to this in a moment.

00:28:57.700 --> 00:29:00.700
We have a PDF download to help with this.

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:13.700
But, however, we must always be on top of
commodity prices and inflation
expectations and know why cyclical
commodities are where they are.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:17.700
This is our interpretation at any one
moment in time.

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:22.700
Are demand factors outstripping supply
factors or vice versa?

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:43.700
And if we have a particular cyclical
commodity where demand factors are very
clearly outstripping supply factors, then,
of course, as traders and portfolio
managers, we're going to find that very
useful as a leading indicator.

00:29:43.700 --> 00:30:03.700
And if supply factors are outstripping
demand factors at any one moment in time,
then it's going to be difficult for us to
use that commodity price as a leading
indicator to potential future economic
growth and stock market direction and
returns.

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:10.700
Now, we have, as I mentioned, a PDF in the
download area.

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:17.700
The name of that PDF is Cyclical
Commodities Demand and Supply Factors.

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:32.700
And in the download, what you'll notice is
there's a whole bunch of sources and
information relating to the oil, copper,
lumber and iron ore markets.

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:50.700
So you can essentially, at any one moment
in time, analyze those factors and gauge
whether demand factors are outstripping
supply factors for the commodity price
move.

00:30:50.700 --> 00:30:55.700
So this is going to be a useful resource
for you going forward.

00:30:55.700 --> 00:31:10.700
So when you see a cyclical commodity price
move, you can use the resource and
determine whether it's a demand-led move
or a supply-led move.

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:18.700
Now, obviously, earlier in this video, we
looked at the CPI and the PPI.

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:22.700
So the consumer price index and the
producer price index.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:29.700
And we're looking here in a bit more
detail now at the PPI All Commodities
Index.

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:35.700
And we're going to look at the
contributory data behind this number.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:53.700
If you look at the screen, you'll see at
