00:37:54.700 --> 00:38:00.700
And you can see, for example, gross rents
across the property sector.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:09.700
You can see non-residential property sales
and leasing with land brokerage fees and
commissions.

00:38:09.700 --> 00:38:19.700
You can also see management fees and you
can see residential property sales and
rentals, brokerage fees and commissions
and also appraisal fees.

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:44.700
So, very obviously, if we're using lumber
as one of our cyclical commodity prices
that we're analyzing, if we get demand-led
inflation or deflation, this can be a good
leading indicator to a particular sector
like real estate services.

00:38:44.700 --> 00:39:04.700
So, services we do not necessarily have or
need the ability to be able to look at
leading indicators that are directly
leading into, for example, real estate
commissions and brokerage fees.

00:39:04.700 --> 00:39:13.700
We know if there's a slowdown in the
housing sector, in the construction
sector, if lumber is falling, for example,
and it's demand-led.

00:39:13.700 --> 00:39:26.700
So, using it in conjunction with our other
leading indicators that this is likely to
feed into the real estate services sector
further down the line.

00:39:26.700 --> 00:39:45.700
So, even though for goods, for all
commodities and the sub-indices for all
commodities is essentially in the
manufacturing sector, there is a very
obvious read-across to many services.

00:39:45.700 --> 00:40:12.700
So, one of the most useful things we can
regularly do as traders or portfolio
managers is to stay on top of cyclical
commodity prices like energy and
industrial metals and determine as a whole
the general level of demand and use this
as a guide to future expected inflation
and GDP growth.

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:16.700
So, we are using it as a leading indicator
gauge.

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:31.700
Now, if you look on the screen on the
left-hand side, you will see the producer
price indices for goods and in goods, all
commodities.

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:42.700
And then you'll see the sub-indices for
all commodities, which are farm products,
processed foods and feeds, and also
industrial commodities.

00:40:42.700 --> 00:40:48.700
And within industrial commodities, you
will see further sub-indices.

00:40:48.700 --> 00:40:49.700
So, you'll see further sub-indices.

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:53.700
So, you'll see, for example, fuels and
related products and power.

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:55.700
You'll see lumber and wood products.

00:40:55.700 --> 00:40:58.700
You'll see metals and metal products.

00:40:58.700 --> 00:41:03.700
And also industrial commodities less
fuels.

00:41:03.700 --> 00:41:27.700
And what we can do is if you look at the
major sub-category, which is industrial
commodities, we can actually, on the FRED
website, click on industrial commodities
and get taken to the page on the
right-hand side, which is the producer
price index by commodity industrial
commodities index.

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:36.700
So, this essentially isolates our
industrial cyclical commodities as a
group, as a whole.

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:51.700
And of course, we can always click in to
the sub-indices like lumber and wood
products, metals and metal products, fuels
and related products and power, industrial
commodities, less fuels.

00:41:51.700 --> 00:42:05.700
We can always click in to those
sub-indices, bring up the pages and
download the data and really drill down
into the cyclical commodity indices
themselves.

00:42:05.700 --> 00:42:18.700
But we look at the headline level, the All
Commodities Index, and we look at
Industrial Commodities Index, which
contributes to the All Commodities Index.

00:42:18.700 --> 00:42:43.700
So, using these indices as a leading
indicator gauge, when cyclical commodities
are viewed as a group, their relative
price performance may show healthy or weak
demand from cyclical industries of the
manufacturing sectors of the US economy.

00:42:43.700 --> 00:42:49.700
Now, prices may also go up or down based
on supply-side issues.

00:42:49.700 --> 00:42:51.700
We mentioned this earlier.

00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:57.700
If there is a constant demand and
tightening supply, prices will go higher.

00:42:57.700 --> 00:43:00.700
That means we're getting inflation, okay?

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:08.700
So, we're getting inflation from a supply
issue, a tightening supply issue.

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:14.700
If there's constant demand, yet ever
increasing supply, prices will go down.

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:16.700
So, we have the opposite.

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:17.700
We have the opposite.

00:43:17.700 --> 00:43:24.700
We have deflation from increases in supply
given a constant level of demand.

00:43:24.700 --> 00:43:44.700
So, if at any point in time, we are
analyzing cyclical commodity prices and
indices, what we have to understand is, is
that if when we do our work, we determine
that the price is moving, whether that
increases or decreases,

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:58.700
if the price is moving because there's a
supply driven issue, we must temper our
ability to gauge future economic growth.

00:43:58.700 --> 00:44:12.700
So, basically, with supply-side driven
inflation, cyclical commodities are going
up in price, not because of an overall
demand increase.

00:44:12.700 --> 00:44:24.700
In this situation, real GDP may actually
fall because demand is constant, and in
this particular cyclical commodity, we
have a supply situation occurring, okay?

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:30.700
We have a supply issue that's causing the
price to go up, and demand is constant.

00:44:30.700 --> 00:44:39.700
So, in the future, the read-across into
real GDP is a small read-across.

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:52.700
On the flip side, with supply-side driven
deflation, the cyclical commodity price is
going down, not because of an overall
demand decrease.

00:44:52.700 --> 00:45:00.700
It's going down because of an oversupply
of the commodity, given a constant level
of demand.

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:09.700
And even in that situation, when the
commodity price goes down, real GDP in the
future may rise more than we expect.

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:31.700
So, when we have supply-side issues in a
particular cyclical commodity, and we
determine at any one time that this is
probably the reason why a cyclical
commodity is either going up in price, so
inflation, or down in price, deflation.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:44.700
The read-across, the read-across, we have
to understand that the read-across in
forecasting real GDP is going to be much
weaker than if it was demand-led.

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:55.700
If it's demand-led, then cyclical
commodity prices are indicative of future
CPI and PPI levels, which is reported
monthly.

00:45:55.700 --> 00:46:08.700
So, monthly inflation numbers, and of
future economic growth, so real GDP
growth, which is reported quarterly, so
expansion and contraction.

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:31.700
So, if at any one moment in time, we
determine that a cyclical commodity price
move is much more down to demand factors
than supply factors, then it has a direct
read-across to economic growth and, of
course, inflation.

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:41.700
And also, because we know cyclical
commodity prices are trading every day,
then they are leading inflation and GDP
stats.

00:46:41.700 --> 00:46:50.700
This is essentially providing us, as
traders and PMs, with another leading
indicator gauge to the economy.

00:46:50.700 --> 00:47:06.700
So, in the downloads area, you will see
the spreadsheet called Commodity Prices,
and you will see all the data for oil,
copper, lumber, and iron ore.

00:47:06.700 --> 00:47:35.700
And you need to go through these
spreadsheets, and look at the commodities
price data, and look at the distribution
of returns analysis, so the statistical
distribution analysis for each one, and
understand the one, two, and three
standard deviation moves on a daily and
weekly basis for each of the commodity
prices.

00:47:35.700 --> 00:47:40.700
Commodity prices, so each of the commodity
products.

00:47:40.700 --> 00:47:58.700
And you need to combine this with the
information and sources in the PDF
document for the corresponding products
for oil, copper, lumber, and iron ore, and
learn about the supply and demand
dynamics.

00:47:58.700 --> 00:48:17.700
Commodity prices, you make a combination
of these resources, and in the future,
when you're analysing commodity prices,
you will understand what a normal move is,
and what an abnormal move is, and you will
understand what's driving the price.

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:36.700
Now, something else you can use as an
indicator for future expected levels of
inflation and growth is the commitment of
traders reports for the cyclical
commodities that we're looking at.

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:43.700
So, let's have a look at what we call the
commitment of traders reports, or cut
reports.

00:48:43.700 --> 00:49:02.700
We can use non-commercial transactions
data from the Commodity Futures Trading
Commission, the CFTC, as an indicator for
professional money manager positioning in
our cyclical commodities.

00:49:02.700 --> 00:49:12.700
So, what this data actually is, is the
real positions, long and short, of the
smart money in the market.

00:49:12.700 --> 00:49:16.700
It's the institutional and hedge fund
money.

00:49:16.700 --> 00:49:19.700
So, when we analyse this, what are we
getting?

00:49:19.700 --> 00:49:30.700
We are getting a sentiment gauge of future
expectations of inflation and GDP growth
from their positioning in cyclical
commodities.

00:49:30.700 --> 00:49:40.700
So, we analyse this data regularly to get
a sense of how the smart money is
positioning itself in the market.

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:52.700
And this data can act, again, as a leading
gauge of future expected inflation, GDP
growth, and general stock market
direction.

00:49:52.700 --> 00:50:03.700
So, specifically, what we want to know is
if the smart money are long or short a
particular cyclical commodity and by what
magnitude.

00:50:04.700 --> 00:50:11.700
Now, these numbers have not fallen from
the magic fairy tree or fallen from the
sky.

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:13.700
They are real numbers.

00:50:13.700 --> 00:50:19.700
These are the real positions, long and
short, of institutional and hedge fund
money.

00:50:19.700 --> 00:50:22.700
They are real numbers.

00:50:22.700 --> 00:50:31.700
Of course, if hedge funds are positioned
long, they are committed to the belief
that the commodity will go up in price.

00:50:31.700 --> 00:50:39.700
Therefore, they are committed to cyclical
commodity inflation and, of course, vice
versa.

00:50:39.700 --> 00:50:44.700
This is why it is called a commitment of
traders report.

00:50:44.700 --> 00:50:59.700
And, by the way, when you look at this
data, what you also have to understand is
that the hedge fund community have been
through the same leading indicator
analysis as you have throughout the PTM
video series.

00:50:59.700 --> 00:51:02.700
They are looking at the same things.

00:51:02.700 --> 00:51:17.700
So, the COT report data is useful because
we can calculate turning points in
positioning in professional trader circles
in the hedge fund community.

00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:39.700
Therefore, we are determining professional
trader or hedge fund sentiment and their
inflation and potential growth
expectations because of the positioning
that they currently have or changing in a
particular cyclical commodity.

00:51:39.700 --> 00:51:49.700
Now, how do we actually go through this
data and determine inflation and growth
sentiment from the hedge fund community?

00:51:49.700 --> 00:51:56.700
Well, in the downloads area, you'll see
the commitment of traders spreadsheet
file.

00:51:56.700 --> 00:52:03.700
And we cover the cyclical commodities
space.

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:06.700
We've got all of our cyclical commodities
in there.

00:52:06.700 --> 00:52:15.700
And you'll see the numbers for each of the
cyclical commodities on the screen here.

00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:18.700
We've looked at copper as an example.

00:52:18.700 --> 00:52:22.700
And you'll see the four columns that we're
looking at.

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:25.700
We're looking at total open interest.

00:52:25.700 --> 00:52:32.700
We're looking at manager longs, manager
shorts, and we're looking at the net long
and short.

00:52:32.700 --> 00:52:35.700
So, what do we do?

00:52:35.700 --> 00:52:50.700
We do our analysis by taking the open
interest percentage change on both long
and short non-commercial hedge fund
positioning and work out the difference
between the two.

00:52:50.700 --> 00:53:05.700
If we minus the percentage short
positioning from the percentage long
positioning, we get the difference between
the percentage of contracts that hedge
funds are long and short, which gives us
the net.

00:53:05.700 --> 00:53:13.700
And what this does is create a new dynamic
that is known in professional trading
circles as the flip.

00:53:13.700 --> 00:53:25.700
The flip basically shows us when hedge
funds flip from committing to being net
long to net short or net short to net
long.

00:53:25.700 --> 00:53:30.700
And it also shows us how committed is the
commitment.

00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:34.700
So, the rate of change by which
positioning is changing.

00:53:34.700 --> 00:53:43.700
Now, if you look at the screen here, as
mentioned, we are using here copper as an
example.

00:53:43.700 --> 00:53:46.700
And you can see our four columns.

00:53:46.700 --> 00:53:55.700
And you can see on the screenshot that
we've taken, we've taken this directly
from the data in the spreadsheet download.

00:53:55.700 --> 00:54:04.700
And we're looking at the period April 2020
to August 2020.

00:54:04.700 --> 00:54:14.700
And you can see we've highlighted two
weeks in the data, which is the week of
the 26th of May and the week of the 2nd of
June.

00:54:14.700 --> 00:54:26.700
And you can see on the right-hand side in
the fourth column, you can see the flip
occurring at this stage.

00:54:26.700 --> 00:54:33.700
So, the flip occurs in the week of the 2nd
of June 2020.

00:54:33.700 --> 00:54:44.700
What this means is, is that the net
positioning of the hedge fund community in
copper has gone from being net short to
net long.

00:54:44.700 --> 00:54:51.700
So, they've gone from being, as the
professional trading community, going from
bearish to bullish.

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:56.700
So, right there in that week, you can see
the flip occurring.

00:54:56.700 --> 00:55:03.700
Now, we can actually chart the commitment
of traders data for the entire history.

00:55:03.700 --> 00:55:07.700
And that's available in the download as
well in the spreadsheet file.

00:55:07.700 --> 00:55:10.700
You can have a look at this in the
spreadsheet.

00:55:10.700 --> 00:55:15.700
And obviously, also look at the data in
all the columns that's feeding this chart.

00:55:15.700 --> 00:55:19.700
And you can see on the screen where the
red arrow is pointing.

00:55:19.700 --> 00:55:33.700
This is where the flip in copper occurred
in the week preceding June 2nd, 2020,
because the report is on the data for the
previous week.

00:55:33.700 --> 00:55:44.700
So, if you think about this chart in its
entirety and think about periods that
we've looked at previously in the PTM
video series.

00:55:44.700 --> 00:55:49.700
Think about the August 18 to August 19
period.

00:55:49.700 --> 00:55:56.700
And think about the period when the Fed
was cutting rates over the summer of 2019.

00:55:56.700 --> 00:56:04.700
And then the lead out of the turnaround in
the economy in the second half of 2019.

00:56:04.700 --> 00:56:05.700
So, think about that period.

00:56:05.700 --> 00:56:14.700
You can see on the commitments of traders
numbers when we look at 2018, we get a
peak.

00:56:14.700 --> 00:56:20.700
And then the hedge fund community is
getting less long in copper.

00:56:20.700 --> 00:56:22.700
So, they are less committed.

00:56:22.700 --> 00:56:25.700
They are committed long, but less
committed.

00:56:25.700 --> 00:56:35.700
And then we get a flip going negative
during that August 18 to August 19 period.

00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:42.700
We bounce around at the 0% bound for a
number of months.

00:56:42.700 --> 00:57:01.700
And then in the spring, summer of 2019,
that's when bearish sentiment or net short
positioning in the commitments of traders
reports or copper accelerates and goes
lower to a trough.

00:57:01.700 --> 00:57:18.700
And then we get a trough coming out of
that trough when it seems the Fed's
actions began working and the commitment
of hedge fund traders, the hedge fund
community gets less short.

00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:23.700
So, they're still committed short, but get
less committed.

00:57:23.700 --> 00:57:33.700
And then we get the flip that we just
looked at where we get a flip positive in
2020 after COVID.

00:57:33.700 --> 00:57:43.700
We did get a flip before where the hedge
fund community did go positive at the end
of 2019, beginning of 2020.

00:57:43.700 --> 00:57:50.700
But COVID was obviously taking hold in the
first quarter of 2020.

00:57:50.700 --> 00:57:56.700
And then we get the flip positive long
back in June 2020.

00:57:56.700 --> 00:58:03.700
And we see this data borne out in prices
as well.

00:58:03.700 --> 00:58:11.700
So, we can look at the copper price and
what was going on at these times visually
on the chart.

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:36.700
So, if we look at our cyclical commodity
copper price during that period, mid 2018
to the end of 2019, you can see the copper
price heading lower as the commitments of
traders, the hedge fund community gets
less and less long and then flip to short
and get bearish.

00:58:36.700 --> 00:58:55.700
And then we have our recovery, where we
know during that period in 2019, where we
had the Fed cutting rates in July,
September, October 2019, we know they
started to take effect.

00:58:55.700 --> 00:59:02.700
The interest rate cuts started to take
effect, our leading indicators were
turning around.

00:59:02.700 --> 00:59:17.700
And then we have COVID, and then COVID
occurs, but then we get our flip
aggressively in the copper price from the
hedge fund community at the beginning of
June.

00:59:17.700 --> 00:59:22.700
So, end of May, beginning of June, 2020.

00:59:22.700 --> 00:59:36.700
So, at any one point in time, guys, for
cyclical commodities, you need to be
looking at the commitments of traders
reports and analyzing the flip.

00:59:36.700 --> 00:59:46.700
The flip is where we tend to have turning
points in cyclical commodities, but you
also need to look at the extremes.

00:59:46.700 --> 00:59:53.700
So, when the gap between positive and
negative sentiment becomes historically
wide either way.

00:59:53.700 --> 01:00:01.700
So, we're looking at the peaks and the
troughs in commitments of traders reports
for cyclical commodities.

01:00:01.700 --> 01:00:12.700
So, when we have extreme positive, it
basically means that longs versus shorts
are at or near historical highs.

01:00:12.700 --> 01:00:22.700
When we have extreme negative, it's
because the longs and shorts, longs minus
shorts, are at or near historical lows.

01:00:22.700 --> 01:00:35.700
So, when we witness these extremities in
cyclical commodities, it can be a signal
or a warning sign of potential turning
points.

01:00:35.700 --> 01:00:39.700
However, guys, there is obviously no
rulebook here.

01:00:39.700 --> 01:00:52.700
So, positioning and prices of a cyclical
commodity can stay at extreme positive or
extreme negative for a long time before
reversing.

01:00:52.700 --> 01:01:04.700
So, don't take this as a rules-based
scenario where, for example, you're trying
to connect things in your mind where you
say,

01:01:04.700 --> 01:01:13.700
if I see, for example, the copper
commitments of traders peaking, then I
should be looking to short copper stocks.

01:01:13.700 --> 01:01:29.700
That's not the case because the definition
here of the positive data from the longs
minus the shorts being at extremes and
becoming slightly less extreme

01:01:29.700 --> 01:01:33.700
So, what I mean is simply the hedge fund
community are still bullish.

01:01:33.700 --> 01:01:36.700
They're just less committed than before.

01:01:36.700 --> 01:01:50.700
So, it's a fallacy to read into this and
say, at, for example, a turning point from
a peak or a trough, I should directly read
across into the stock market and say,

01:01:50.700 --> 01:01:59.700
I'm going to go short or long, for
example, copper stocks or construction
stocks from lumber.

01:01:59.700 --> 01:02:02.700
It's obviously, it doesn't work like that,
guys.

01:02:02.700 --> 01:02:17.700
It's not a rulebook where you take
positions simply based on the flip or the
extremities becoming slightly less extreme
in the commitments of traders reports

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:21.700
and directly reading across into positions
in stocks.

01:02:21.700 --> 01:02:26.700
So, definitely do not do that.

01:02:26.700 --> 01:02:42.700
You have to do the proper work, which is
looking at the actual demand and supply
dynamics as well to see if this is the
demand led or supply led in terms of price
movement.

01:02:42.700 --> 01:02:58.700
What you're doing with the cyclical
commodities commitments of traders reports
is looking at sentiment and looking at it
in the context of your work on the demand
and supply dynamics.

01:02:58.700 --> 01:03:20.700
And if you're seeing evidence that a
commodity price move, a cyclical commodity
price move, if you're seeing evidence that
it is demand led and you're seeing
evidence from the hedge fund community
that they are seeing it too and they are
getting committed one way or the other,
